"""Additional scenario analysis, organized by success criterion."""

DECISIONS = {
'3.1.1': 'Consider a localized single-page application where the navigation stays in English but the main article changes to Spanish. The default language should reflect the predominant content of the current page; identify the English navigation separately if appropriate. A hard-coded English root can remain hidden during visual review because the text itself looks correct. Inspect after the content change and again after loading the translated URL directly. A passing check requires a programmatically determinable language that reflects the actual content; a language dropdown or a flag is not a substitute. Language support varies across synthesizers, so document both the markup result and the voice used rather than treating an absent voice as proof that the attribute is wrong.',
'3.1.2': 'Consider a travel article with a French greeting, a personal name, and a technical product name in the same paragraph. The French greeting may require a language change; the name and technical term need contextual review because the criterion contains exceptions. Do not make the entire paragraph French merely to accommodate the greeting. Conversely, marking only the first word of a complete French quotation can cause the remaining words to inherit the English page language. Inspect the final rendered markup after any translation or content-management updates, then read across the boundary. The observation to record is which passage has which inherited language, including whether the surrounding language resumes.',
'3.1.3': 'Imagine documentation says that a user must “revoke a token” without explaining either term. A useful explanation identifies the token as a credential and explains that revocation makes it unusable; it also tells the user the practical consequence for an existing integration. Simply replacing “revoke” with “invalidate” may exchange one unfamiliar term for another. Present the explanation close to the instruction or supply a reliable glossary mechanism, and make sure the link text identifies the term. Ask a novice what will happen to requests using the token after revocation. Their answer can reveal whether the definition communicates the intended restricted meaning rather than merely repeating the same unfamiliar word.',
'3.1.4': 'Suppose an application uses “SSO” in a navigation label and defines single sign-on only in an introductory article. A learner landing directly on a settings page may never encounter that introduction. An accessible glossary, nearby expansion, or equivalent mechanism provides another way to identify the expansion in the relevant context. Check a narrow screen and a touch device, where hover-based hints may be unavailable. If a visible label is abbreviated for space, verify that a reader can still locate the expansion without guessing which icon to activate. Distinguish this requirement from defining a specialist concept: spelling out the words may still leave the concept itself in need of explanation.',
'3.1.5': 'A useful comparison exercise is to give two readers the original policy and the supplemental version separately, then ask each to identify eligibility, required documents, and the deadline. Record differences in their answers, and check omissions against the source policy. A diagram can show the sequence of steps, but its labels, relationships, and meaningful content need an accessible text equivalent. Maintain both versions when the policy changes; a stale plain-language explanation can cause harm even if it is easy to read. Remember that the criterion specifies a reading-ability threshold and permits supplemental content or a qualifying version: shorter text alone does not establish that the condition has been met.',
'3.1.6': 'Suppose an audio pronunciation button sits beside an ambiguous word. Its accessible name should identify what will play, such as “Hear pronunciation of record,” rather than only “Play.” A keyboard user must be able to operate it and a listener must be able to distinguish the relevant pronunciation. Provide a readable guide or explanation where sound is not available to the learner. Evaluate whether the spelling is genuinely ambiguous in that particular sentence: a guide may not be necessary where the context already communicates meaning, while an isolated vocabulary list can require more explanation. Review the accuracy of any phonetic notation and explain the notation if the intended audience may not recognize it.',
'3.2.1': 'A second failure pattern occurs in a form that opens a new window whenever a field receives focus. A learner tabbing through the form may be repeatedly interrupted even though they have made no choice. Replace that behavior with a labeled Help button, and make the new window or dialog behavior clear as appropriate. Test with a pointer too, because clicking a field can also place focus there. Note that focus can intentionally be moved by a script, such as focusing an error summary after submission; assess whether receiving focus itself initiates a further context change. A focus indicator is feedback about location and is not, by itself, the disruptive transition addressed here.',
'3.2.2': 'Consider a checkbox labeled “Send updates” that submits and reloads the entire form as soon as it is checked. A user exploring the option may lose their place or unsubmitted answers. Add an explicit Save button, preserve the other values, and verify the checkbox can be changed in both directions before saving. If the application instead updates an in-page preview while keeping focus and location stable, evaluate whether it actually changes context rather than assuming all automatic updates fail. Provide accurate instructions before any control whose input causes a consequential context change. Test the real interaction on keyboard and touch because browser behavior for select controls can vary.',
'3.2.3': 'Compare three pages that all contain Home, Courses, and Help. The sequences Home–Courses–Help and Home–Resources–Courses–Help preserve the repeated links’ relative order; Courses–Home–Help does not. A learner may use visual memory, screen-reader navigation, or repeated Tab presses to locate these items, so inspect the order presented through each relevant path. When a user chooses a customized order, do not mistake their choice for an unexplained inconsistency. Test any shared component change across several page types and responsive breakpoints; a fix to the desktop header can leave a separate mobile menu unchanged.',
'3.2.4': 'Compare a download action on a lesson page and a resource page. If both save the same kind of file, naming one “Download PDF” and the other only “Arrow” makes the second action harder to recognize, especially without the icon. Give repeated functionality identification that communicates the same purpose, including the accessible name. Conversely, if two similar icons have different outcomes, labels should distinguish those outcomes rather than forcing them to sound identical. Check translated versions as well as the original language; consistent identification concerns the function in context, not literal repetition of English words on every localized page.',
'3.2.5': 'A timeout that moves the learner from a reading page to a survey may be triggered without deliberate action. Offer a way to prevent that transition before it happens, such as a clearly labeled automatic-advance setting, and verify that the setting controls the actual timer. If the user explicitly activates a Next link, record that user action as the trigger. A background refresh of a small score display is a different case from redirecting the browser or moving focus to another view; examine its actual effect. Test the disabled state for the duration promised and make sure the learner can still advance when ready.',
'3.2.6': 'For an audit, choose a public course page, a lesson page, and an assessment page in the same set. If each repeats the same chat support and help-center links, write down their relative position to other repeated page components. A mobile menu can look different while still preserving a consistent sequence, but verify the order by opening it and navigating. If the user rearranges their own dashboard, record that as a user-initiated change. Avoid concluding that a page fails simply because it offers no additional contact method: the criterion focuses on relative order when the specified help mechanisms are repeated.',
'3.3.1': 'A useful error summary might say “There are two errors: Email address: enter a value; Date: enter a valid date.” Each link can move the learner to the relevant field. Test that the individual field also has the corresponding message and that the summary does not become stale after one error is corrected. Keep the text distinct from a general server outage message, which should not falsely blame a particular input. A red border or warning icon may reinforce the message visually, but color or shape alone cannot identify both the affected item and the problem in text. Preserve previously entered values during correction.',
'3.3.2': 'A shipping form may ask for an address, a postal code, and a country. Make clear which fields are required and provide format guidance only where it applies; a rigid example that is valid for one country may mislead someone using another. Associate each label with its input rather than relying solely on visual proximity. For related checkboxes, a fieldset and legend can provide the shared question while each checkbox retains its own label. Inspect the browser accessibility tree and tab through the form: the learner should hear enough information before entering a value and should not need to erase a placeholder to find the expected format.',
'3.3.3': 'Compare two messages for a quantity field: “Invalid” and “Enter a whole number from 1 to 10.” The second suggests a correction when the range is known and disclosure is safe. Test a non-number and a number outside the range, and confirm the advice is accurate for both. Retain the original input and let the learner edit it; automatically replacing it with a guessed value can conceal an unintended choice. For authentication, revealing which part of the credentials was wrong can create security concerns, so a general message may be appropriate. Error identification and a safe, known correction suggestion work together but answer different questions.',
'3.3.4': 'Suppose a form deletes user-controlled data after a confirmation step. A review mechanism should show which data is affected and offer a way to cancel or correct the selection. A generic OK button does not establish what will be lost. Alternatively, if deletion is reversible, verify that a restoration path really restores the data and make any meaningful limitation clear. If input checking is the selected safeguard, demonstrate a detectable error and an opportunity to fix it. Test the actual submission path, not just the presence of an extra screen. The applicable scope and the chosen safeguard determine what evidence a reviewer needs.',
'3.3.5': 'For an address field, help might explain where to find an apartment number and whether it belongs on a separate line. A help page about all account settings may be useful generally but require the learner to search for this specific answer. Link to the precise relevant section and keep critical guidance visible where possible. Test at a narrow viewport, with keyboard focus and with text zoomed, to make sure the help is reachable and does not cover the form permanently. After returning from help, verify the earlier form entries remain intact. Update the help when backend rules or accepted address formats change.',
'3.3.6': 'A review screen can support a profile update by showing the proposed values and providing an Edit action before Save. Verify that the learner can change one field without losing the rest, and that the final submission uses the revised value. If a system instead provides Undo, check the saved state after the reversal and a refresh. If relying on input checking, ensure a detected error produces a chance to correct it rather than silently rejecting or discarding the submission. The enhanced criterion applies the specified prevention mechanisms more broadly than the related AA criterion; it does not prescribe the same design for every form.',
'3.3.7': 'An address offered for reuse may later become invalid because the learner changed it or it was supplied in an earlier, separate process. Review whether the previously provided information is still valid and whether the request belongs to the same process. When reuse is appropriate, present an editable choice and show what will be submitted. Test a person who needs a separate billing address; the interface must not silently force the delivery address into the billing record. Security can justify re-entry in some circumstances, but record why that specific information must be entered again rather than using a generic security claim for every field.',
'3.3.8': 'For a one-time code, test whether the learner can paste the code or use supported autofill on the actual device. If the UI divides the code into several boxes, verify that a pasted complete code is handled correctly rather than forcing a digit-by-digit transcription. Check the subsequent recovery route too. If an alternative is offered, the learner needs to reach it without first completing the inaccessible test it is intended to replace. The minimum criterion includes specified exceptions for object recognition and personal content; those are separate from the general options of an alternative or assisting mechanism and should not be generalized to every challenge.',
'3.3.9': 'A recovery process that asks a user to select their own picture may use personal-content identification, an exception available at the minimum level but absent at the enhanced level. Examine whether it functions as a cognitive test and whether a usable alternative or assisting mechanism is provided. Likewise, a passkey can be a valuable alternative, but check enrollment, device availability, and recovery instead of evaluating only the sign-in button. Test every mandatory factor in sequence. Record which route a learner can actually complete and what assistance it provides; an inaccessible fallback can leave a user locked out even when the ordinary route is well designed.'
}
